# Add Transatlantic Troy — Fifth Persona for siliconandstone.com

## Background

Silicon and Stone uses reader personas to tag briefings and help readers filter content by the perspective closest to their work. The site currently has four personas. We are adding a fifth: **Transatlantic Troy**.

The "Find Your Perspective" section on `/briefings` already has `lg:grid-cols-5` on the persona card grid, so the layout is prepared for five cards.

---

## Persona Definition

| Field | Value |
|-------|-------|
| **Name** | Transatlantic Troy |
| **Slug/Key** | `troy` |
| **Role** | Founder / CEO |
| **One-liner** | Decoding European compliance standards to compete across the Atlantic. |
| **Short label (filter button)** | Troy |
| **Mobile abbreviation** | T |

### Who Troy represents

Troy is a US or Canadian founder/CEO running a startup or mid-market company (not a Big Tech giant) that is doing business or wants to do business in Europe. He reads Silicon and Stone to understand how digital sovereignty — the AI Act, data localisation, cloud certification, procurement rules — changes the cost and complexity of doing business across the Atlantic.

His question when reading a briefing: *"What does this mean for my European market entry, and what do I need to do differently because of it?"*

Troy reads primarily through the **Institutional lens** (obligations, exposures, decisions) but from a transatlantic market-entry angle. Where Compliance Clara is already inside a European operation managing regulatory deadlines, and Industrial Ian is running existing European supply chains, Troy is on the outside looking in — assessing whether the market is worth entering and what the sovereignty landscape will cost him to navigate.

